  "worked_solution": "Using the transformed equation, we solve for $Y(s)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{2}Y(s)-2s+Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{s} \\\\ ({s}^{2}+1)Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{s}+2s \\\\ Y(s) & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s({s}^{2}+1)}+\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the inverse transform, we first use partial fraction decomposition on the algebraic part:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{s({s}^{2}+1)}=\\frac{A}{s}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+1}\n$$\n\nSolving for the constants, we find $A=1$, $B=-1$, and $C=0$, giving:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{s({s}^{2}+1)}=\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the inverse Laplace transform. Let \n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1},\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nf(t)=1-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(t).\n$$\n\nAccording to the second shifting theorem, \n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}{{e}^{-as}F(s)}={u}_{a}(t)f(t-a).\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={L}^{-1}{{e}^{-3s}(\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1})}+{L}^{-1}{\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}+1}} \\\\ y(t) & ={u}_{3}(t)(1-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(t-3))+2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\ny(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(t)+u(t-3)(1-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(t-3)).\n$$",
